

## MATHieu functions

The above expansion is not limited to integral values of \(r\), and it is a very good approximation for \(r\) of the form \(n+\frac{1}{2}\) where \(n\) is an integer. In case of integral values of \(r\)=\(n\), the series holds only up to terms not involving \(r^{*}\)=\(n^{*}\) in the denominator. Subsequent terms must be derived specially (as shown by Mathieu). Mulholland and Goldstein [20.38] have computed characteristic values for purely imaginary \(q\) and found that \(a_{0}\) and \(a_{2}\) have a common real value for \(|q|\) in the neighborhood of \(1.468\); Bouwkamp [20.5] has computed this number as \(q_{0}=\pm i\)\(1.46876852\) to \(8\) decimals. For values of \(-iq>\)\(-iq_{0}\), \(a_{0}\) and \(a_{2}\) are conjugate complex numbers. From equation 20.2.25 it follows that the radius of convergence for the series defining \(a_{0}\) is no greater than \(|q_{0}|\). It is shown in [20.36], section 2.25 that the radius of convergence for \(a_{2n}(q)\), \(n\geq\)2 is greater than \(3\). Furthermore

\[a_{r}-b_{r}=O(q^{r/r-1}),\,r\rightarrow\infty\,.\]

**Power Series in \(q\) for the Periodic Functions (for sufficiently small \(|q|\))**

\[\begin{split} ee_{0}(z,q)&=2^{-\frac{1}{2}}\bigg{[} 1-\frac{q}{2}\cos 2z+q^{2}\left(\frac{\cos 4z}{32}-\frac{1}{16}\right)\\ &\hskip 19.916929pt-q^{2}\left(\frac{\cos 6z}{1152}\frac{11 \cos 2z}{128}\right)+\ldots\bigg{]}\end{split}\]

\[\begin{split} ee_{1}(z,q)&=\cos z-\frac{q}{8}\cos 3z \\ &\hskip 19.916929pt+q^{2}\left[\frac{\cos 5z}{192}\frac{\cos 3z}{64} \frac{\cos z}{128}\right]\\ &\hskip 19.916929pt-q^{2}\left[\frac{\cos 7z}{9216}\frac{\cos 5z}{1152} \frac{\cos 3z}{3072}+\frac{\cos z}{512}\right]+\ldots\end{split}\]

\[\begin{split} ee_{1}(z,q)&=\sin z-\frac{q}{8}\sin 3z \\ &\hskip 19.916929pt+q^{2}\left[\frac{\sin 5z}{192}+\frac{\sin 3z}{64} \frac{\sin z}{128}\right]\\ &\hskip 19.916929pt-q^{2}\left[\frac{\sin 7z}{9216}+\frac{\sin 5z}{1152} \frac{\sin 3z}{3072}\frac{\sin z}{512}\right]+\ldots\end{split}\]

